name  = mat4xvec4
shape = 20,000 vertices through one 4x4 matrix, 5000 passes
args  = 20000
cflags = -ffp-contract=off
claim = THE ROW WHERE EXPLICIT FLOATING-POINT LANES PAY. Until this one the
        suite could not show that: matmul is a fixed-order reduction and is
        unchanged by range-check versioning because floating-point addition
        does not reassociate, and axpy_simd loses to the auto-vectorizer
        because two lanes per step is a quarter of the work in flight. The
        justification for explicit SIMD was carried entirely by utf8valid_simd,
        on byte lanes.
        ---
        A vertex transform is the same multiply and add as matmul and it does
        NOT need any license to reorder, because the sum runs down the columns:
        r = c0*v.x + c1*v.y + c2*v.z + c3*v.w. Each of the four output lanes
        accumulates column 0, 1, 2, 3, which is the order the scalar program
        accumulates them in too. So bench_simd.mere is not an approximation of
        bench.mere -- it is the same arithmetic, and the two print the same
        bytes. That identity is the point of the row and the runner enforces
        it: a change that makes the lanes faster by changing the answer fails
        here rather than looking like a win.
        ---
Measured at v0.1.444 on arm64 (Apple clang 21), median of five:
        scalar 206 ms, f64x2 143 ms, C 201 ms, Rust 340 ms, Go 280 ms. The
        lanes are 1.44x the scalar Mere program and ahead of C.
        ---
        THE DISASSEMBLY SAYS SOMETHING BETTER THAN "clang cannot vectorize
        this", and it is worth reading before trusting the number. Both Mere
        loops are vectorized; they differ in WHICH AXIS IS IN THE LANES. clang,
        given the scalar program, puts TWO VERTICES in the lanes: the versioned
        kernel opens with `ld4.2d {v28,v29,v30,v31}` -- a de-interleaving load
        that lands (x0,x1) (y0,y1) (z0,z1) (w0,w1) -- and does the transform as
        16 fmul.2d and 12 fadd.2d for the pair. But the accumulator is a sum
        ACROSS vertices in a fixed order, so with vertices in the lanes that
        sum is back in the lane direction: the loop then spends four `mov
        d9, v8[1]` lane extracts and EIGHT SCALAR fadd putting the pair back
        together. That is matmul's problem, reintroduced by the choice of axis.
        The explicit program puts the four COMPONENTS in the lanes instead, so
        accumulating across vertices is lane-parallel and nothing is unpacked:
        23 instructions, 8 fmul.2d, 8 fadd.2d, two of which are the
        accumulators, and not one scalar floating-point instruction.
        ---
        Note what this does NOT say. Per vertex the two loops are almost the
        same SIZE -- 21.5 instructions against 23 -- so the 1.44x is not an
        instruction count, and anyone reporting it as one is reporting a
        coincidence. What the lane version removes is the unpack and the
        scalar tail on the accumulator's dependency path. That reading is the
        disassembly's, and the disassembly is committed above; the attribution
        of the milliseconds to it is not separately proven here.
        ---
        The C row is the scalar program, which is what a C programmer writes
        without intrinsics -- the same comparison utf8valid_simd makes. clang
        vectorizes it the same way it vectorizes Mere's scalar row, and its hot
        loop carries the same eight scalar fadd. A C row written with NEON
        intrinsics would be the Mere lane row and is not what this measures.
        ---
        The size is deliberately CACHE-RESIDENT: 20,000 vertices is 640 KB, and
        the passes are what make the row long enough to time. This row is about
        arithmetic throughput, and a working set that did not fit would measure
        the memory system instead -- which is what axpy is for. The number is
        therefore "this kernel with its data in cache" and not a claim about
        streaming a real vertex buffer.
        ---
        Same floating-point discipline as matmul, for the same reasons. Unfused
        everywhere: Mere emits `#pragma STDC FP_CONTRACT OFF` itself, the C row
        carries -ffp-contract=off above, the Go row carries the float64()
        conversions its spec names, and Rust does not contract without an
        explicit mul_add. Inputs are non-dyadic (divided by 1000, not 1024) so
        the arithmetic actually rounds. The answer is printed as the two halves
        of the IEEE-754 pattern, because a formatted double hides the low bits.
        Four accumulators rather than one: a single running sum would be one
        serial dependency chain and would time the latency of an add.
        ---
        WASM, WHERE THE NUMBER IS MEMORY AND NOT TIME. That backend has no
        optimizer behind it -- the text is assembled and run as written -- so
        the same two programs are recorded here the way note-196's SIMD rows
        are: by where they run out of memory. Counted in the emitted WAT, the
        scalar kernel does 32 `f64.store` and 65 bump-allocator advances PER
        VERTEX, because on Wasm every double that crosses a call or a data
        structure is a heap box and this loop carries four accumulators and
        four components. The lane kernel does ZERO f64.store, zero v128.store
        and ONE bump advance: since v0.1.429 a SIMD value stays in a v128 local
        unless it escapes, and here nothing does. So on Wasm the explicit lanes
        do not pay a boxing tax, they AVOID one -- the opposite of what the
        f64x2 rows on this backend usually show. Measured out-of-memory
        thresholds at 10,000 vertices: the scalar row survives 17 passes and
        fails at 18, the lane row survives 75 and fails at 80 -- 4.4x further.
        That ratio is smaller than the 65:1 in static allocations would
        predict, so something outside this kernel is allocating too; it is
        recorded as measured and not explained here.
        ---
        No Node, Ruby or Python row. The kernel is 28 floating-point operations
        per vertex over an unboxed array of doubles, and none of the three has
        one -- a JavaScript Float64Array row would be a fair fight, but the
        Ruby and Python rows would be timing their boxed-float allocators and
        would take minutes. What this row exists to compare is one language's
        two spellings of the same arithmetic against native code.

# Two Vecs: the 16-element matrix and 4 x 20,000 = 80,000 doubles of vertices,
# 640 KB, built by vec_push with growth in place (v0.1.414). Nothing else
# allocates -- the kernel's accumulators are locals and the loop is a tail
# call. Set from the measurement below at v0.1.444; a reading under the floor
# means the meter broke, which is the failure a one-sided band cannot see.
#
# 1,048,952 B at v0.1.444, and IDENTICAL for the scalar and the f64x2 rows --
# which is itself the check that the C backend keeps f64x2 in a register
# (clang's vector_size(16)) rather than boxing it, the way Wasm and RISC-V do.
# 640 KB of vertices rounded up to the 1 MiB the Vec's growth lands on, plus
# 376 B. The ceiling is set under twice the reading so that doubling garbage
# cannot come back unannounced.
alloc_min = 786432
alloc_max = 1310720
